  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions

  #sol-item(1, "C", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Allocation of scarce resources among competing wants. Profit, tax and share prices are three small applications of that central problem, not the problem itself.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "C", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    *Should* makes it a value judgement: it recommends rather than describes, so no figure can prove it wrong. Positive statements describe what is and can be tested.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "C", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    No scarcity, so no sacrifice, so no opportunity cost. A zero price with scarcity — a rationed good — is the trap; the test is scarcity, not price.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "B", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Profit, and it is the residual: the entrepreneur receives what is left after the other three factors are paid. Rent belongs to land, interest to capital and wages to labour.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    The price of the good is the one variable that moves the buyer along the curve. A shift needs a change in a determinant such as income, tastes or population.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "B", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Fertiliser is an input, so it acts on the producer. The other three act on buyers and move the demand curve — the stems of three common distractors.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "D", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Both changes push the price down, so the price must fall. Quantity could move either way, because one change raises it and the other lowers it.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "C", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Below equilibrium, quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied, so the queue replaces the price and some buyers pay above the ceiling illegally. A surplus belongs to a minimum price, not a maximum.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "A", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    A subsidy lowers the cost of production, so supply shifts right, price falls and quantity rises. A tax does the same thing in the opposite direction.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "A", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    A vertical supply curve cannot move, so the whole adjustment falls on price. The quantity sold stays at the fixed amount and buyers bid the price up.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "A", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Set 80 − 2P = 20 + 2P, so 60 = 4P and P = ₦15; then Qd = 80 − 30 = 50. The other three pairs fail when substituted back.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "C", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    The good's own price moves buyers along the curve; it never shifts it. The other three are determinants of demand and shift the curve.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "B", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Quantity rises 20 per cent and price falls 10 per cent, so PED = 20 ÷ 10 = 2.0. Dividing the other way gives 0.5, the commonest error in the chapter.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "D", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Quantity supplied does not respond at all, so the line stands at one quantity whatever the price. A horizontal supply curve is the perfectly elastic case.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "D", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Elastic demand means quantity rises proportionately more than price falls, so the quantity effect outweighs the price effect and revenue rises.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "A", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    XED = −5 ÷ +10 = −0.5. The negative sign is the definition of complements: one good dearer, the other bought less.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "A", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    The law is a short-run statement: it describes what happens as more of a variable factor is added to a fixed factor. In the long run every factor can change, so the law has nothing to say.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "D", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    TC = TFC + TVC = 200 + (30 × 10) = ₦500. Adding the two figures without multiplying by output gives ₦230.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "B", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Break-even means TR = TC: 40Q = 300 + 25Q, so 15Q = 300 and Q = 20. Dividing the fixed cost by the price alone gives 7.5, which is below the answer.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "A", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Economies of scale are a long-run idea: the firm grows, all factors change, and average cost falls. Rising marginal cost is the short-run law of diminishing returns.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "C", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Perfect competition requires identical products, so differentiation is exactly what is absent. Differentiation is the defining feature of monopolistic competition.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "D", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    MR = 60 − 2Q; setting MR = MC gives 60 − 2Q = 10, so Q = 25. Setting price equal to marginal cost — the competitive rule — gives 50, which is the trap.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "C", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Output stops where MR = MC, short of the point where price equals marginal cost, so some mutually beneficial trades never happen and the surplus is lost.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "D", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Final goods, produced within the country, valued at market prices. Sales of second-hand goods and intermediate goods are excluded, and citizens' income abroad belongs to GNP.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "A", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    GNP = GDP + net factor income from abroad — the earnings of residents from abroad minus the earnings of foreigners at home.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "D", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Real GDP is valued at constant prices, so a rise means more output rather than higher prices. Nominal GDP can rise while output stands still.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "A", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Income per head: national income divided by total population. Dividing by the labour force gives income per worker, which is a different and much larger figure.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "A", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    A pension is a transfer payment: money changes hands but nothing is produced. The other three are payments for currently produced goods or services.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "B", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Comparing values is the unit-of-account function. Paying for goods is the medium of exchange; keeping wealth is the store of value.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Open market operations — buying and selling treasury bills — change the reserves of the banks. Tax and government spending are fiscal instruments, not monetary ones.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "D", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Multiplier = 1 ÷ reserve ratio = 1 ÷ 0.2 = 5. Using the ratio itself as the multiplier gives 20, and halving it gives 2.5.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "B", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    General and persistent: one good's price is not inflation, and a rise in the money supply is a cause rather than the definition.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "D", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Too much money chasing too few goods. Rising input costs are cost-push inflation, which is the other side of the same chapter.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "A", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    A fixed income buys less as prices rise, and the loss cannot be recovered. Debtors gain, because the real value of their debt falls.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "C", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Moving between jobs is frictional unemployment, and it is short-lived. A mismatch of skills is structural, and a slump in demand is cyclical.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "D", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Inflation against unemployment, in the short run. The trade-off weakens in the long run, when expectations adjust — the point the examiner likes to test.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "B", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Fiscal policy works through taxation and government spending. The other three are monetary instruments operated by the central bank.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "A", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Progressive, because the average rate rises with income. A regressive tax takes a larger share from the poor, and a proportional tax takes the same share from everyone.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "C", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    The Central Bank of Nigeria sets the monetary policy rate and manages the money supply. The Ministry of Finance handles fiscal policy, and the Bureau publishes the data.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "B", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    7.5 per cent, the rate that has applied since 2020. Five per cent is the old rate, and 15 per cent is the standard rate in several other countries.
  ]

